germany's new government is starting its  first full day in office with a packed  agenda that includes dealing with a  health system strained by the fourth  wave of the coronavirus pandemic the new  chancellor olaf schultz convened his  three-party cabinet for the first time  on wednesday evening  schultz a social democrat will lead a  17-member cabinet with the  environmentalist green party and the  market friendly free democrats earlier  he took up his post in the chancelley  building when outgoing chancellor angela  merkel officially handed over to her  successor after 16 years in power  schultz served for four years under  meckle as vice chancellor and finance  minister  the mutual respect between the old and  new german leaders was apparent in the  warm words between them and what many  have described as a textbook democratic  handover of power  congratulations to your chancellor dear  olaf shouts  i know from my own experience that it is  a moving occasion to be elected to this  office  as you perhaps can imagine  it is an exciting fulfilling job and a  demanding one but if you approach it  with enthusiasm then it's perhaps one of  the most beautiful jobs there is  to bear responsibility for this country  i sincerely wish you good luck and every  success for our country  i think it's something very special to  be federal chancellor of this republic  it's a great challenge  i'm very grateful that the citizens of  this country and the german bundestag  have given me the mandate to do this  the fact that you and i were able to  share so many experiences together will  help me fulfill my role  thank you again for the work you've done  [Applause]  earlier we spoke to our correspondent  nina haza she sent us this assessment  olaf schwarz's first day in his new  position as germany's new chancellor is  a full one of course he has to deal with  a couple of domestic issues tonight  he'll attend the consultations between  the 16 regional leaders here in germany  who will sit down and discuss the  current and also future possible  anti-covet measures because of course  it's vital also for this new government  to tackle this fourth wave of the corona  pandemic and then there are a couple of  organizational events all of schultz  attends because the handover process  from angela merkel's team to his has to  be completed but today also sees olaf  charles jump onto the international  stage in his new role for the very first  time attending the virtual democracy  summit convened by u.s president joe  biden now olaf schultz is a firm  believer in the idea of multilateralism  and in  jointly fighting authoritarianism and  corruption worldwide he's also promised  to help uphold human rights worldwide  there are a lot of international crises  that give him no time to waste which is  why his first official visits will take  him on friday to paris and brussels  where events on the ukrainian border and  the dispute between the us and russia  are going to be on the table  new foreign minister is in brussels for  the latest stop of her diplomatic tour  on her first day in the job and alina  bairbach is in the belgian capital to  meet with the european union's foreign  policy chief and nato officials she  began the day in paris with her french  counterpart and will later travel to  poland she has promised to take a  tougher line with authoritarian states  ms bierbach told journalists that there  was plenty to discuss ahead of this  weekend's meeting of g7 foreign  ministers  many topics are on the table we have the  situation on the border between belarus  and poland  the current situation  in ukraine  then the further deployment of troops on  the ukrainian border and of course then  on to clarification of the position and  any action europe will take ahead of the  g7 summit  i'm joined now by our brussels bureau  chief alexander von nahman  alexandra it's the first full day in  office for the new foreign minister and  elena barbach she's visited paris  brussels is next what's on her agenda in  the eu capital today  well the first thing on her agenda is a  meeting with eu foreign policy chief  joseph burrell that is taking place  in the building right behind me and we  had a chance to catch up with the german  foreign minister before the meeting and  i asked her  what is the biggest foreign policy  challenge for berlin and for the  european union from her perspective and  she told us that she thinks that it's  very important right now for the  european union to be united and to speak  with one voice when it comes to the many  challenges the block is facing for  example the crisis at the eu's external  border with belarus or  russia's military buildup at the border  with ukraine and that is probably the  topic that  is going to be on the agenda during her  meeting with nato secretary general  general stoltenberg later today  bearbach has already talked about  ensuring that climate change is a  priority what do her counterparts in the  eu expect from her continuity or change  well she had indicated in the past that  she would like germany to take a tougher  stance on russia and china so i can  imagine that her counterparts here in  brussels would like to hear more about  that but overall the signals that  brussels has got from the new german  government are pointing to continuity in  german foreign policy and that is  something that many here will feel  reassured by because of course everyone  knows how big the role is that germany  is playing in the european union and  many are hoping for continuity in german  foreign policy  many thanks alexander that's alexandre  von naaman dw brussels bureau chief
 

